00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:04.160 align:start position:0%
the crowds are back on the streets in  delhi  after seven weeks stores in one of  india's biggest markets southern bazaar  reopened on monday  traders say they could not be more  relieved  everybody of course is very happy  because at one  one part we were losing our livelihood  and also living in  the fear of coronavirus so definitely  each and every trader of the market is  very happy that finally  both things are on the better side  like many other indian states delhi  imposed a strict lockdown in april in an  attempt  to slow down a devastating second wave  of covet 19.  those restrictions have reduced the  number of new cases of the virus quite  considerably  but they've also meant a substantial  drop in people's incomes  the reopening in delhi is taking place  just a few days after the latest  economic figures showed india's gross  domestic product or gdp  dropped by 7.3 percent over the past  year its worst performance since records  began  70 years ago  more than 230 million indians have been  pushed into poverty in the past year  but migrant workers who lost their jobs  when lockdowns were imposed  are returning to the cities  the cases are not as high as they were  that's why we have come back  to earn money to feed ourselves there  aren't many ways to do that in the  villages  however as restrictions are eased state  leaders are preparing for a possible  third wave of the virus  as per their calculations we are  preparing for a peak of 37 000 cases a  day  we are calculating that if we reach this  peak how many beds and intensive care  units would be required  and out of them how much oxygen will be  needed and the number of medicines that  will be required  health experts say the most urgent task  for india is to get more people  vaccinated and while the government  addresses the shortage of doses it has  available  it's also being urged to make sure basic  covet 19 protocols including social  distancing and wearing masks  remain in place we need to ensure that  super spreader events  which have been the most dangerous  pathways  for the viral spread are strictly  avoided both through public resolve  as well as through administrative  vigilance  uh till possibly december this year  india's official death toll from the  pandemic is 350  000 though health experts say the actual  figure is likely to be  much higher as the country emerges from  what's widely described as the worst  crisis in its modern history health  experts say it's imperative the lessons  learned  are now applied elizabeth puranam al  jazeera new delhi
 

